# November 19, 2026: Franklin and Grand Isle counties, Vermont (region vtn, Northern Vermont & Champlain Valley), first pass.
# Drawn at random by tools/choose.py. The Champlain Islands (the Hyde log cabin, the stone courthouse at North Hero,
# James Ritchie's stone church and school on Isle La Motte, Fisk Quarry, St. Anne's Shrine, the South Hero Inn, the
# Alburgh railroad pump house) and the Missisquoi valley inland (Highgate Falls' iron bridge and brick church, St. Albans'
# railroad headquarters and 1793 tavern, the Jewett brothers' covered bridges, Enosburg's opera house, Brigham Academy,
# Fletcher's union church and the Chester Arthur birthplace site).
# Facts come from Wikipedia articles read through tools/wiki_raw.py (each summarising its National Register form),
# HMdb marker texts and the managing bodies' own pages read through tools/page.py, as linked on each place.
from data_b import N
from data_ma import W
BATCH={'key':'vtn_jkrm','label':'Franklin and Grand Isle counties, Vermont','added':'2026-11-19 08:57'}
LCLT=['Lake Champlain Land Trust: Fisk Quarry Preserve','https://lclt.org/fisk-quarry-preserve']
JEW='Built by the brothers Sheldon and Savannah Jewett of Montgomery, who cut the timber at their own sawmill on West Hill; their bridges round Montgomery are the largest group in Vermont known to be by one builder.'
PLACES=[
N('hyde-log-cabin','Hyde Log Cabin','Grand Isle','vtn','landmark','standing',44.72619,-73.29197,'exact','open','drive','','1783–',
  'Jedediah Hyde Jr., who surveyed the islands for Ira and Ethan Allen, built this cabin of peeled cedar logs in 1783 and raised ten children in it; his family lived in it for 150 years. It is held to be one of the oldest log cabins in the country. In 1946 it was saved from demolition and moved about two miles to this lot (the Vermont Historical Society says it did the moving), and it has been restored several times since.',
  'One room of cedar logs 14 to 18 inches thick, about 20 by 25 feet, with a loft. The big stone chimney is a 20th-century rebuilding of the original. A small wood-frame schoolhouse of 1814 stands on the same lot. Interior: open as a museum in season (below); what is shown inside not checked.',
  'A 1783 cedar-log cabin that one family lived in for 150 years.',
  'Owned by the state and run as a museum by the Grand Isle Historical Society, on the east side of US Route 2 just north of the Grand Isle elementary school. Wikipedia gives it as open on weekends from May to October; the society\'s own hours page was not found, so this season\'s hours are not checked. Park on the lot (not checked on the ground).',
  [W('Hyde Log Cabin'),['Vermont Historical Society: Hyde Log Cabin','https://vermonthistory.org/hyde-log-cabin']],brick='no'),
N('grand-isle-county-courthouse','Grand Isle County Courthouse','North Hero','vtn','civic','standing',44.81875,-73.28977,'exact','view','drive','','1824–',
  'Grand Isle County was set up in 1802 with North Hero as its seat from 1804; its courts first sat in Jed Ladd\'s hotel and tavern. In 1824 the county built this courthouse of island limestone, one of the oldest courthouses left in Vermont and the only stone one. It served at first as court, jail, town hall and church; the town and church moved to a new meeting house next door in 1865. The Scottish mason James Ritchie may have built it, though no document says so.',
  'Two stone storeys under a hip roof, with an octagonal belfry topped by a gold onion dome, a portico of Tuscan columns over the door and a round-arched window above it. A wood-frame jailer\'s house of the early 20th century is joined on by an ell. Interior not checked.',
  'Vermont\'s only stone courthouse, with a gold onion dome over City Bay.',
  'County building at 3677 US Route 2 in North Hero village, on a rise on the west side of the road. Seen from the road; whether and when the public may go in not checked.',
  [W('Grand Isle County Courthouse')],brick='no'),
N('rutland-rr-pumping-station-alburgh','Rutland Railroad Pumping Station','Alburgh','vtn','rail','standing',44.97466,-73.30998,'site','view','drive','','c. 1903–1940s',
  'The Rutland Railroad pushed north from Burlington in the late 1890s, took over the crossing from Alburgh to Ogdensburg, New York in 1897 and built a large railyard at Alburgh, of which little is left. About 1903 it built this small pump house to lift lake water to the yard for its steam engines; it also supplied part of the town. Once diesel replaced steam it fell out of use, and the town bought it from the railroad in 1954.',
  'A small brick building with a gabled roof on a stone foundation at the lake\'s edge, its openings boarded up; brick infill shows where round pipe holes once were. Inside (not open) the register form recorded a mid-20th-century gasoline motor, electric motor and water pump. The town\'s modern water plant stands nearby. The photograph on its Wikipedia article shows red brick walls, boarded windows and a metal roof.',
  'The pump house that watered the Rutland\'s steam engines at Alburgh.',
  'Owned by the Town of Alburgh, at the west end of Lake Street on the shore of Lake Champlain. Look from the street; the building is boarded shut. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Rutland Railroad Pumping Station')],brick='yes'),
N('south-stone-school-isle-la-motte','South Stone School House','Isle La Motte','vtn','civic','standing',44.85189,-73.34385,'exact','open','drive','','c. 1842–',
  'A district school built about 1842 or 1843 of the island\'s own dark limestone, sold in the trade as "black marble", probably by the Scottish mason James Ritchie, who is credited with at least six other buildings left on the island. It taught the town\'s children until the schools were consolidated about 1930. The Isle La Motte Historical Society bought it in 1932 and has kept it as its museum ever since.',
  'One storey of rough-cut and squared limestone with small stones packed into the gaps in the Scottish way, a wide stone chimney beside the door, and inside one room with pine wainscoting holding the island\'s history. The society asks visitors to count the 480-million-year-old fossils in the outside walls. The society\'s campus also holds the Duba blacksmith shop of about 1890 and the Frances Ford slab-log cabin.',
  'A Scottish mason\'s schoolhouse with fossils in its walls.',
  'Isle La Motte Historical Society, at Main Street and Quarry Road, about five miles from the Isle La Motte bridge. The society\'s page asks for volunteer docents for Saturdays in July and August, so that is when the museum is staffed; other opening times not checked. Parking not checked.',
  [W('South Stone School House'),['Isle La Motte Historical Society','https://islelamotte.us/services/historical-society/']],brick='no'),
N('old-stone-church-isle-la-motte','United Methodist Church (Old Stone Church), Isle La Motte','Isle La Motte','vtn','worship','standing',44.87692,-73.33756,'exact','view','drive','','1843–',
  'Isle La Motte\'s Methodists met in houses until the Scottish stonemason James Ritchie built them this church in 1843 on land given by Ira Hill, whose stone house Ritchie also built; it is thought his finest work. The upper floor was the church and the basement was split between a school and the town meeting room until 1892. A congregation formed by merging several denominations owns it now.',
  'Coursed grey limestone laid in lime mortar, two bays by three, with a two-stage wooden tower and stained glass in square-headed windows. The 1843 date is cut in the stone lintel over the door. Interior not checked.',
  'The finest of the island\'s Scottish-built stone buildings.',
  'A working church on the north side of Church Street, east of the Four Corners in the village. See it from the street; service times not checked.',
  [W('Methodist Episcopal Church of Isle La Motte')],brick='no'),
N('st-annes-shrine-isle-la-motte','Saint Anne\'s Shrine and the site of Fort Sainte Anne','Isle La Motte','vtn','worship','standing',44.8998,-73.34726,'site','open','drive','','1666–',
  'In 1666 the French built Fort Sainte Anne on Isle La Motte to guard Montreal against the Iroquois, with a chapel to Saint Anne; the first Catholic Mass in Vermont was said here, and the Bishop of Quebec came in 1668 to baptise Iroquois converts. The fort was gone some time between 1670 and 1690. The Bishop of Burlington bought the land in 1892 to save it and a small chapel was built; the first director, Father Joseph Kerlidou, dug part of the site and found knives, buttons and pottery, French and older Native work.',
  'The fort itself is gone: the Way of the Cross on the grounds marks where it stood. Today there is a chapel, a grotto, statues, reflection gardens, a beach, a memorial walk on local history and a statue of Samuel de Champlain; some of the excavated finds are shown on site. Interior of the chapel not checked.',
  'Where the French built a fort and said Vermont\'s first Mass, in 1666.',
  'Run by the Edmundite Fathers with the Catholic Diocese of Burlington, on Shrine Road off US Route 2 a few miles south of the Canadian border. Wikipedia says the grounds are open to the public all year and gives daily Mass at 11:15 in July and August and the cafe and history room open Memorial Day to Columbus Day; the shrine\'s own page is drawn by script and could not be read, so these hours are not checked against it.',
  [W('Saint Anne\'s Shrine'),['Desany, "Enshrining the Past: The Early Archaeology of Fort St. Anne" (Journal of Vermont Archaeology, 2006)','https://www.vtarchaeology.org/wp-content/uploads/v7_ch3_reduced.pdf'],['Seven Days: a bike ride around Isle La Motte (2020)','https://www.sevendaysvt.com/arts-culture/vermonting-a-bike-ride-around-isle-la-motte-turns-up-history-religion-and-very-old-rocks-30753872/']],th=[44.89912,-73.34798],thn='Lot on Saint Annes Road (OpenStreetMap)',brick='?'),
N('fisk-quarry-isle-la-motte','Fisk Quarry','Isle La Motte','vtn','industry','traces',44.84639,-73.36208,'site','public','walk','','late 1700s–1990s',
  'A limestone quarry cut into the Chazy Fossil Reef, one of the oldest reefs on Earth. Seven Days, a Burlington weekly, writes that quarrying here began in the late 18th century, making it the oldest quarry in Vermont, and that the island\'s black limestone went into the U.S. Capitol and the National Gallery of Art (one local source, not checked elsewhere). When drilling started again in August 1995, Linda Fitch of the neighbouring Fisk Farm gathered a coalition of land trusts to buy it, and it became a preserve in 1999 and a National Natural Landmark in 2009.',
  'The worked quarry walls and floor, now wet and green, with fossils of the reef (gastropods and others) in the cut rock and a very short interpretive trail. The Fisk Farm next door, where Vice President Theodore Roosevelt was staying on September 6, 1901, has a historic marker (Seven Days).',
  'Vermont\'s oldest quarry, cut into a 480-million-year-old reef.',
  'Owned and managed by the Isle La Motte Preservation Trust, with the Lake Champlain Land Trust holding the easement. Open dawn to dusk all year, free. Gravel lot marked by the Fisk Quarry sign on the lake side of West Shore Road. No motor vehicles or bicycles on the preserve. The Goodsell Ridge preserve two miles north has more fossil trails and a visitor centre open some summer days.',
  [LCLT,['Lake Champlain Land Trust: Fisk Quarry guide (2016)','https://www.lclt.org/wp-content/uploads/2016/08/FiskQuarryGuide-August-2016-Update.pdf'],['Lake Champlain Land Trust: Goodsell and Fisk Quarry preserves','https://www.lclt.org/goodsell-and-fisk-quarry-preserves'],['Seven Days: a bike ride around Isle La Motte (2020)','https://www.sevendaysvt.com/arts-culture/vermonting-a-bike-ride-around-isle-la-motte-turns-up-history-religion-and-very-old-rocks-30753872/']],conf='mixed',th=[44.84481,-73.36185],thn='Fisk Quarry Preserve lot, West Shore Road',brick='no'),
N('south-hero-inn','South Hero Inn','South Hero','vtn','hotel','standing',44.64557,-73.30292,'exact','view','drive','','1829–1970s',
  'Built in 1829 at the crossroads of South Hero, on the old main road from Burlington to Canada, and kept as an inn for travellers until the early 1970s; in the later 19th century it took in tourists and people come for the island\'s mineral springs.',
  'Two and a half storeys of squared, hammered stone blocks under a gabled roof, with a two-storey porch on the US 2 front, a Federal fan in its pediment, and a second formal front on South Street with a porch of turned posts. A wood-frame ell runs off the west side. It now holds a bank branch (Wikipedia); interior not checked.',
  'An 1829 stone inn on the road to Montreal.',
  'A bank branch at 301 US Route 2, at the corner of South Street in South Hero village. See it from the road or go in during the bank\'s business hours (not checked).',
  [W('South Hero Inn')],brick='no'),
N('highgate-falls-lenticular-bridge','Highgate Falls Lenticular Truss Bridge','Highgate','vtn','landmark','standing',44.93484,-73.04729,'site','public','walk','','1887–',
  'Built in 1887 by the Berlin Iron Bridge Company of Connecticut to the Douglas and Jarvis patent for a "parabolic" or lenticular truss, which used less iron than ordinary trusses. At 215 feet it is one of the longest of its kind left in the Northeast, and one of the first Vermont bridges built with state help. It carried cars until 1976 and reopened for people on foot in 2000 after the state\'s historic bridge program restored it (Gribble Nation).',
  'The lens-shaped iron trusses with their overhead web of rods, portal posts with finials and the company\'s ornamental plate and cresting, on stone abutments, plus an 80-foot lenticular pony truss on the south approach. It crosses the Missisquoi just below the dam at Highgate Falls.',
  'A lens-shaped iron bridge from 1887 you can walk across.',
  'Owned by the State of Vermont (Division for Historic Preservation), open to people on foot. It runs from the end of Mill Hill Road toward VT Route 207 (Highgate Road), where the state\'s 2002 marker stands at Baker Road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Douglas & Jarvis Patent Parabolic Truss Iron Bridge'),['HMdb: Highgate Falls Lenticular Truss Bridge','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=109270'],['Vermont State Historic Sites: historic bridges','https://historicsites.vermont.gov/historic-bridges'],['Gribble Nation: Highgate Falls parabolic truss bridge (2019)','https://gribblenation.org/2019/05/travel-new-england-highgate-falls.html']],brick='no'),
N('st-johns-episcopal-highgate-falls','St. John\'s Episcopal Church, Highgate Falls','Highgate','vtn','worship','standing',44.93026,-73.04811,'site','view','drive','','1829–',
  'Built in 1829–30 by Joel Whitney of Enosburg on the village green, and consecrated in 1834 by John Henry Hopkins, the first Episcopal bishop of Vermont. The plan follows one in Asher Benjamin\'s pattern book The Country Builder\'s Assistant, with Gothic touches added. It was a full parish until 1896 and has since been looked after by Holy Trinity in Swanton.',
  'A one-storey brick church with Gothic-arched windows either side of a projecting entry, a fanlight over the double door, a Palladian window above, a Federal louvred fan in the gable and a square tower turning to an octagonal belfry. Interior not checked.',
  'An 1829 church from a pattern book, with Gothic windows.',
  'An Episcopal church on the east side of the green in Highgate Falls. See it from the green; when it is open not checked.',
  [W('St. John\'s Episcopal Church (Highgate Falls, Vermont)')],brick='?'),
N('central-vermont-railroad-hq-st-albans','Central Vermont Railroad Headquarters','St. Albans','vtn','rail','standing',44.81105,-73.08535,'site','view','drive','','1860s–1920s',
  'The Central Vermont was put together in 1860 from several shorter lines and ran from Montreal across Vermont to Boston, New London and later New York; St. Albans native J. Gregory Smith, its first president, is said to have chosen his home town for its headquarters. The yards and shops here, built from the 1860s to the 1920s, are the largest group of railroad buildings in Vermont. The four-track passenger station west of the office was torn down in 1963.',
  'The 1870s headquarters office on the north side of Lake Street, Second Empire with a pair of mansard-roofed towers in red brick (Wikipedia\'s photograph), still offices of the New England Central Railroad; north of it a switch house of about 1900 that is now the Amtrak station, and a large roundhouse beyond. South of Lake Street the old shops and sheds are now used by other businesses. Interiors not checked.',
  'The office towers of the railroad that ran Vermont from St. Albans.',
  'Working railroad offices and active tracks: see the buildings from Lake Street, Federal Street and the Amtrak platform, and stay off the tracks and yards.',
  [W('Central Vermont Railroad Headquarters')],brick='yes'),
N('hathaways-tavern-st-albans','Hathaway\'s Tavern','St. Albans','vtn','hotel','standing',44.8227,-73.08088,'exact','view','drive','','1793–',
  'Silas Hathaway, an English immigrant, built this tavern in 1793 on his farm on North Main Street, with sawn wood shipped up the lake from a mill at Whitehall, New York. It is the oldest building in St. Albans. From 1797 to 1801 the Franklin County court met in it, and it was for a time the post office. Dr. Julius Hathaway bought it in 1805 and made it a house; his descendants kept it until 1931.',
  'A two-storey hip-roofed clapboard house on a low knoll above the road, with modillions under the eaves and a Colonial Revival portico over a door with sidelights. Inside, the register form describes the wide central halls of the tavern; it is a private house, so the interior is not open.',
  'The oldest building in St. Albans, where the county court first sat.',
  'A private home at 255 North Main Street near the north city line. View from the street only.',
  [W('Hathaway\'s Tavern')],brick='no'),
N('longley-covered-bridge','Longley Covered Bridge','Montgomery','vtn','landmark','standing',44.90724,-72.65565,'exact','view','drive','','1863–',
  'The oldest of the Jewett brothers\' surviving bridges, built in 1863 across the Trout River. '+JEW+' It was fully restored in 1992.',
  'A Town lattice bridge 84.5 feet long, with vertical board siding stopping short of the eaves, a standing-seam metal roof and stringers added under the plank deck. Wikipedia says it now leans to one side with the trusses bowed in the middle, and is closed and bypassed by a temporary bridge beside it.',
  'The Jewetts\' oldest bridge, leaning and closed.',
  'Town of Montgomery bridge on Longley Bridge Road, just west of VT Route 118. Closed to traffic: see it from the temporary bridge or the road. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Longley Covered Bridge')],brick='no'),
N('comstock-covered-bridge-montgomery','Comstock Covered Bridge','Montgomery','vtn','landmark','standing',44.89966,-72.64464,'exact','public','drive','','1883–',
  'Built in 1883 across the Trout River just west of Montgomery village. '+JEW+' Blow & Cote of Morrisville renovated it completely in 2003.',
  'A one-lane Town lattice bridge 69 feet long on dry-laid stone and concrete abutments, with vertical board siding and white-painted portal ends.',
  'A Jewett lattice bridge at the edge of Montgomery village.',
  'Town road: Comstock Bridge Road, south of VT Route 118. Open to traffic. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Comstock Covered Bridge (Montgomery, Vermont)')],brick='no'),
N('hutchins-covered-bridge','Hutchins Covered Bridge','Montgomery','vtn','landmark','standing',44.85859,-72.61254,'exact','view','drive','','1883–',
  'Built in 1883 over the South Branch of the Trout River. '+JEW+' A butter tub company here once used it heavily; later it was all but abandoned and held up with steel beams. Alpine Construction of Schuylerville, New York, rebuilt it in 2009.',
  'A one-lane Town lattice bridge 77 feet long with an open strip under the eaves, a wooden plank deck and dry-laid stone abutments, one end set on a big rock outcrop. The approach is little more than a one-lane gravel drive.',
  'A Jewett bridge saved from collapse in 2009, on a dead-end lane.',
  'Hutchins Bridge Road, a dead-end road a short way west of VT Route 118. Whether the road is town-kept to the bridge not checked; houses may be near, so do not block the lane.',
  [W('Hutchins Covered Bridge')],brick='no'),
N('hopkins-covered-bridge','Hopkins Covered Bridge','Enosburgh','vtn','landmark','standing',44.92061,-72.67294,'exact','view','drive','','1875–',
  'Built in 1875 across the Trout River by the Jewett brothers of Montgomery. '+JEW+' The state found it over-stressed and closed it in 1993; Renauld Bros. of Vernon renovated it and it reopened in 1999.',
  'A one-lane Town lattice bridge 91 feet long with vertical board siding, an open strip below the roof and a wooden plank deck.',
  'A Jewett bridge that serves one farm.',
  'On Hopkins Bridge Road just west of VT Route 118 in northeast Enosburgh. Wikipedia says the road gives access to a single farm, so treat the far side as private and look from the road end. Parking not checked.',
  [W('Hopkins Covered Bridge')],brick='no'),
N('east-fairfield-covered-bridge','East Fairfield Covered Bridge','Fairfield','vtn','landmark','standing',44.78608,-72.86239,'exact','public','drive','','c. 1865–',
  'Built about 1865 by an unknown builder across Black Creek, it is Fairfield\'s only 19th-century covered bridge left. After years of trying to keep it open the town had to close it to all traffic; Blow & Cote rebuilt it from August 2008 and it reopened on July 5, 2009.',
  'A one-lane queen post bridge 68 feet long with vertical iron rods in the trusses and a web of iron rods overhead, a plank deck and dry-laid stone abutments finished in concrete and mortar.',
  'Fairfield\'s last old covered bridge, a queen post span.',
  'Town road: Bridge Street at the west end of East Fairfield village, a short way south of VT Route 36. Parking not checked.',
  [W('East Fairfield Covered Bridge')],brick='no'),
N('chester-arthur-birthplace','President Chester Arthur State Historic Site','Fairfield','vtn','landmark','standing',44.8358,-72.85959,'site','open','drive','','1903–',
  'Chester A. Arthur, the 21st president, was born in Fairfield in 1829, where his father was a preacher. A granite monument was put up in 1903 on the spot thought to be his birthplace, and in 1953 a replica of the house was built from an old photograph. The state runs it as a historic site.',
  'The 1953 replica house with exhibits, the 1903 granite monument, and walking trails; Wikipedia says visitors can also see the nearby Fairfield Baptist church where his father preached. The house is a replica, not the original.',
  'A president\'s birthplace, rebuilt from a photograph.',
  'Vermont State Historic Site, 4588 Chester Arthur Road, Fairfield, on a gravel road: from Fairfield village go north and bear right after about a mile, then about five miles more; from VT Route 108 about two miles west. Closed now for the 2026 season; Wikipedia gives tours on weekends from July to mid-October; the 2027 dates are not checked. Parking across Chester Arthur Road. Pets on leash on the grounds and trails, not in the building. No restrooms.',
  [W('Chester Alan Arthur State Historic Site'),['Vermont State Historic Sites: Chester Arthur hours and directions','https://historicsites.vermont.gov/chester-arthur/hours-admissions-directions']],brick='no'),
N('enosburg-opera-house','Enosburg Opera House','Enosburg Falls','vtn','civic','standing',44.90872,-72.8021,'exact','view','drive','','1892–',
  'B. J. Kendall, a local pharmacist who grew rich selling remedies for horse ailments, had his company build this hall in 1892 and give it to the town, formally in 1894. Town meeting has been held in it ever since, and it has stayed a stage for the region, with John Philip Sousa among its early guests.',
  'A big clapboarded wood-frame hall on a high stone basement with a tall square corner tower that stands over the village, and a gable-on-hip roof. Interior not checked.',
  'The opera house a horse-liniment fortune gave to the town.',
  'Town building at 123 Depot Street in Enosburg Falls, used for shows and town meeting. See it from the street; you can go in when a performance is on (event listings), other times not checked.',
  [W('Enosburg Opera House')],brick='no'),
N('brigham-academy','Brigham Academy','Bakersfield','vtn','civic','standing',44.78377,-72.80266,'exact','view','drive','','1878–',
  'Peter Bent Brigham, a Bakersfield boy who made his fortune in Boston, left his home town $30,000 for schooling when he died in 1877 (his name is also on what became Brigham and Women\'s Hospital). The town spent over $7,000 on this building, opened in 1878, and kept the rest as an endowment. It drew students from across northern Vermont as a high school until 1966, then served as a middle school from 1968 to 1986.',
  'A two-storey brick building thirteen bays wide under a hip roof, with a central tower rising three and a half storeys to a mansard top with clock faces on three sides, and granite sills and lintels. The photograph on its Wikipedia article shows red brick with many windows boarded up (date of the photograph not checked). The interior is mostly from a 1901 addition and renovation; not open as far as checked.',
  'A Second Empire academy with a clock tower, paid for by a Boston fortune.',
  'Beside the town\'s modern school on Academy Lane, set back from VT Route 108 in Bakersfield village. See it from the lane; current use and access not checked.',
  [W('Brigham Academy')],brick='yes'),
N('fletcher-union-church','Fletcher Union Church','Fletcher','vtn','worship','standing',44.6825,-72.91167,'site','view','drive','','1871–',
  'Built in 1871 by the master builder N. R. Bingham to replace the town\'s first union church of 1830, across the road; both were shared by several congregations. A Universalist group is the last known to have worshipped here, in 1899. The town took the building over in 1908 as a community hall, used since for meetings, suppers and the Grange.',
  'A clapboarded wooden church on a rise at a bend in Cambridge Road, with a square front tower and a louvred belfry under a low pyramid roof. Inside, the register form records a gallery and a stage added in 1919. Interior not checked.',
  'A shared country church the town took over as its hall in 1908.',
  'Town-owned community house on Cambridge Road east of Fairfax Road, Fletcher. See it from the road; open for town events only as far as checked.',
  [W('Fletcher Union Church')],brick='no'),
]
